WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.042 --> 00:00:06.711
I wrote this journal entry in 2013.

00:00:10.465 --> 00:00:13.260
"I've been sleeping restlessly for months.

00:00:14.135 --> 00:00:17.180
There's a constant feeling
of tension in my body.

00:00:18.807 --> 00:00:21.643
I love ministry as a Catholic priest,

00:00:21.685 --> 00:00:24.020
the opportunity to share
my faith with people

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.898
and support them as we journey
through life together.

00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:31.236
But I have a desire to share my life
with another person.

00:00:33.071 --> 00:00:35.115
I knew, in discerning ministry,

00:00:35.156 --> 00:00:38.785
that celibacy, not getting married,
was part of the package.

00:00:39.869 --> 00:00:42.289
But I don't know
if I can do this any longer.

00:00:43.873 --> 00:00:47.752
I'm rarely alone,
but I feel bitterly lonely."

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.591
I wrote this when I was on the cusp

00:00:52.632 --> 00:00:57.887
of making one of the biggest
and most significant decisions of my life:

00:00:57.929 --> 00:01:00.348
whether to leave ministry
as a Catholic priest.

00:01:02.392 --> 00:01:06.187
I think my life has been
a reasonably unique and unusual one.

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:10.567
Now in my early forties,
I've had careers as an opera singer,

00:01:10.609 --> 00:01:12.402
a Catholic priest,

00:01:12.444 --> 00:01:14.321
a corporate lawyer,

00:01:14.362 --> 00:01:15.989
and now, a management consultant.

00:01:15.989 --> 00:01:19.367
(Laughter)

00:01:19.409 --> 00:01:22.245
And when people
hear the careers that I've had,

00:01:22.287 --> 00:01:24.164
the most common question I get asked --

00:01:24.205 --> 00:01:25.957
maybe the one that's on your mind --

00:01:25.957 --> 00:01:28.460
is, “What on earth is
the connection amongst it all?”

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:29.586
(Laughter)

00:01:30.503 --> 00:01:32.756
Through all of these changes,

00:01:32.797 --> 00:01:34.924
through all of these careers,

00:01:34.966 --> 00:01:37.594
one constant has been present:

00:01:37.636 --> 00:01:39.429
the practice of reflection.

00:01:40.847 --> 00:01:42.599
Now people may think that reflection

00:01:42.599 --> 00:01:45.185
is something that happens
in a dark, private room

00:01:45.226 --> 00:01:46.853
by candlelight or flashlight,

00:01:46.895 --> 00:01:49.564
to purge one's deepest, darkest secrets.

00:01:50.732 --> 00:01:53.109
But I want to bring
this practice out of the dark

00:01:53.109 --> 00:01:54.277
and into the light.

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:58.573
I want to share how this practice
can help our everyday lives,

00:01:58.615 --> 00:02:00.617
and especially our work lives.

00:02:01.785 --> 00:02:06.039
I want to share how this practice
helps to improve performance,

00:02:06.039 --> 00:02:09.626
to make better decisions,
both big and small,

00:02:09.668 --> 00:02:11.920
and to build better relationships.

00:02:13.546 --> 00:02:17.550
Interestingly, in a 2020 survey
of some 4,000 respondents,

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.886
conducted by BCG and BVA,

00:02:19.886 --> 00:02:21.680
the question was asked,

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.432
"What makes a good leader?

00:02:24.432 --> 00:02:27.268
What are their main qualities and skills?"

00:02:28.019 --> 00:02:31.898
Notably, possessing
a good capacity for reflection

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:35.944
was listed as one of the top five skills,

00:02:35.985 --> 00:02:42.283
the others being empathy, listening,
consideration and team development.

00:02:44.536 --> 00:02:46.621
Reflection is about learning.

00:02:47.372 --> 00:02:50.917
It's about looking at the events
of our lives without judgment,

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:53.044
but with a critical lens.

00:02:54.003 --> 00:02:56.089
I really like the way
leadership professors

00:02:56.089 --> 00:02:59.134
James Bailey and Scheherazade
Rehman describe it:

00:02:59.175 --> 00:03:03.263
"It requires taking an honest moment
to look at what transpired,

00:03:03.304 --> 00:03:05.932
what worked, what didn't,

00:03:05.974 --> 00:03:08.101
what can be done and what can't.

00:03:09.519 --> 00:03:11.604
Reflection requires courage.

00:03:12.188 --> 00:03:14.733
It's thoughtful, and it's deliberate."

00:03:16.025 --> 00:03:17.819
So how do we do this, then?

00:03:18.194 --> 00:03:19.863
Let's take a look at sports.

00:03:19.863 --> 00:03:21.322
I grew up in Australia,

00:03:21.322 --> 00:03:24.534
and one of the most popular
games played was cricket.

00:03:24.576 --> 00:03:29.414
Consistent high performance from batters,
bowlers and fielders is essential.

00:03:29.414 --> 00:03:30.915
But given it’s a team sport,

00:03:30.957 --> 00:03:34.794
it’s not just about
how ... the individual performs,

00:03:34.836 --> 00:03:37.589
but about how the team performs
collectively together.

00:03:39.215 --> 00:03:43.970
Cricketers reflect during a game,
after a game and over time.

00:03:44.721 --> 00:03:47.432
During a game, they might think
about a missed catch

00:03:47.474 --> 00:03:50.810
or how they could bat or bowl better
the next delivery.

00:03:51.352 --> 00:03:55.231
After a game, they might come together
to watch video replays,

00:03:55.273 --> 00:03:58.151
to look at what worked and what didn't,

00:03:58.193 --> 00:04:00.737
which might differ
from what they actually experienced

00:04:00.737 --> 00:04:02.572
during the game itself.

00:04:02.614 --> 00:04:07.160
And then, over time, they might look
to the patterns of their wins and losses,

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.747
to glean even more meaningful
conclusions and insights.

00:04:12.165 --> 00:04:15.502
The same process can and should
be used in the workplace,

00:04:15.502 --> 00:04:19.047
and I don't think we need to watch
video replays of our meetings

00:04:19.047 --> 00:04:20.632
to dissect what took place.

00:04:20.673 --> 00:04:21.800
(Laughter)

00:04:21.841 --> 00:04:24.636
I don't know about you,
but that might feel kind of creepy.

00:04:24.677 --> 00:04:25.678
(Laughter)

00:04:26.638 --> 00:04:30.475
Reflection can be done
through a variety of different formats:

00:04:30.517 --> 00:04:32.268
purposeful thinking,

00:04:32.268 --> 00:04:36.105
written journal entries,
audio notes, pitches,

00:04:36.105 --> 00:04:39.234
a discussion with a mentor
or honest friend.

00:04:39.818 --> 00:04:43.071
The point is to find what works for you

00:04:43.112 --> 00:04:45.240
and to make a regular commitment.

00:04:46.032 --> 00:04:49.494
To examine the events
and experiences of your lives --

00:04:49.536 --> 00:04:52.330
what worked, what didn't, and why --

00:04:52.372 --> 00:04:54.791
and then, to think about what and how

00:04:54.833 --> 00:04:57.293
you would like to do things
differently next time.

00:04:58.711 --> 00:05:04.050
When we practice reflection as a habit,
we gain even more meaningful insight,

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:08.930
because we see patterns that reflection
on stand-alone events doesn't provide.

00:05:09.931 --> 00:05:12.892
I truly think reflection
can help everyone --

00:05:12.892 --> 00:05:16.771
people in every industry,
at every stage in one's career,

00:05:16.813 --> 00:05:19.399
and in every point in one's life.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:24.904
Let me share with you an example
that I think most people can connect with.

00:05:25.613 --> 00:05:27.574
Most of us have meetings.

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:31.077
I was due for a daily catch-up
with a colleague.

00:05:31.119 --> 00:05:34.497
He had just led a client meeting
for the first time.

00:05:34.497 --> 00:05:36.666
It was to give a progress update

00:05:36.666 --> 00:05:40.003
on the work stream
he was responsible for in this project.

00:05:41.504 --> 00:05:44.549
He said to me that the meeting
had gone terribly.

00:05:45.258 --> 00:05:47.677
He didn’t get through the actions taken.

00:05:47.677 --> 00:05:51.973
He wasn’t able to discuss the obstacles
faced or the decisions required.

00:05:52.015 --> 00:05:56.144
He didn’t get to the next steps
or the responsible persons.

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:59.397
He felt

00:05:59.397 --> 00:06:02.066
angst, uneasy, upset.

00:06:03.151 --> 00:06:07.280
He was concerned about what the client
had thought of him and the meeting,

00:06:07.322 --> 00:06:08.781
but more importantly,

00:06:08.781 --> 00:06:11.784
what the client felt
about the work that was going on

00:06:11.826 --> 00:06:13.286
in the work stream.

00:06:14.787 --> 00:06:17.624
It would have been so easy for him
to have pushed past this,

00:06:17.665 --> 00:06:20.043
to try and suppress the emotions,

00:06:20.043 --> 00:06:23.129
but that would have missed
a massive opportunity.

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:26.925
We took a few moments to think

00:06:26.966 --> 00:06:29.802
and objectively reflect
over what took place,

00:06:29.802 --> 00:06:31.888
and then to put in place some commitments

00:06:31.888 --> 00:06:34.891
as to what and how he could do things
differently next time.

00:06:36.434 --> 00:06:38.436
He decided that in [the] future,

00:06:38.478 --> 00:06:41.189
he would start each meeting
with an agenda alignment,

00:06:41.189 --> 00:06:44.359
to make sure there was clarity
on what needed to be achieved.

00:06:44.692 --> 00:06:48.529
And then, he'd resolve to make sure
that he would take greater control

00:06:48.571 --> 00:06:49.948
over the meeting

00:06:49.948 --> 00:06:53.326
so that if topics came up
beyond the scope of the agenda,

00:06:53.326 --> 00:06:55.995
that he’d note that a separate
discussion should be had.

00:06:57.997 --> 00:07:01.501
Reflection helps to improve performance.

00:07:04.045 --> 00:07:06.839
Reflection helps to make better decisions.

00:07:06.839 --> 00:07:08.174
Imagine, for a moment,

00:07:08.216 --> 00:07:10.635
you've been in your current role
for five years.

00:07:10.635 --> 00:07:12.720
It's a creative role,

00:07:12.762 --> 00:07:15.014
but you don't quite feel
you've got that zing,

00:07:15.056 --> 00:07:16.557
that energy for it, anymore.

00:07:17.308 --> 00:07:20.728
You've been offered
another opportunity in the organization.

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:24.565
It’s actually a promotion.
It’s a more senior role, managerial.

00:07:24.607 --> 00:07:28.528
But you'll have responsibility
for looking after 12 direct reports.

00:07:30.613 --> 00:07:33.658
A competitor has also
recently reached out to you.

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:35.326
They've offered you an opportunity

00:07:35.368 --> 00:07:38.121
for the same kind of role
that you've got currently,

00:07:38.162 --> 00:07:40.039
but it pays a higher salary.

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:43.459
There's a big difference

00:07:43.501 --> 00:07:45.962
between being a creative
and being a manager.

00:07:46.796 --> 00:07:49.590
So what is it that really makes you happy?

00:07:50.008 --> 00:07:52.385
What is it that really
makes you fulfilled?

00:07:54.012 --> 00:07:56.889
Reflection provides
a treasure trove of data

00:07:56.889 --> 00:07:58.599
to help you work through this.

00:07:59.684 --> 00:08:02.937
Have your reflections mentioned
being bored with projects,

00:08:02.979 --> 00:08:05.106
or do you just want to try something new?

00:08:06.274 --> 00:08:07.817
Would you like to be a manager?

00:08:07.859 --> 00:08:10.862
Would you like to see people grow
and form and develop them?

00:08:11.279 --> 00:08:14.240
Do you think you could do
a better job than your own manager?

00:08:16.534 --> 00:08:19.203
Reflection provides great insight.

00:08:19.996 --> 00:08:22.874
It's easy to get lost
when you've got an opportunity

00:08:22.915 --> 00:08:25.418
of a fancier job title and more money.

00:08:26.586 --> 00:08:31.049
But reflection enables you
to focus on what really matters,

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:33.009
and to make better choices.

00:08:34.844 --> 00:08:37.388
Let me share with you a final example.

00:08:38.639 --> 00:08:41.017
Most of us have
relationships in our work --

00:08:41.017 --> 00:08:44.687
bosses, customers,
clients, suppliers, whomever.

00:08:46.064 --> 00:08:50.610
And I think most of us try to have
good relationships with these people.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:52.862
If I'm honest,

00:08:52.862 --> 00:08:58.117
while I strive for this ambition,
I haven't always succeeded,

00:08:58.117 --> 00:09:01.746
but reflection has helped me
to build better relationships.

00:09:02.955 --> 00:09:04.165
A number of years ago,

00:09:04.165 --> 00:09:06.959
I was giving a feedback session
with a colleague,

00:09:07.001 --> 00:09:08.461
and after having done so,

00:09:08.503 --> 00:09:11.589
I took a few moments
to jot down some thoughts

00:09:11.589 --> 00:09:13.174
as to how it went.

00:09:15.051 --> 00:09:17.678
I realized I had been too clinical.

00:09:18.054 --> 00:09:22.100
In fact, if only you could have seen
the expressions on my colleague's face.

00:09:22.141 --> 00:09:23.267
(Laughter)

00:09:24.185 --> 00:09:26.896
I'd raced through their various strengths

00:09:26.938 --> 00:09:30.316
and moved onto spending more time
in their areas for development.

00:09:31.025 --> 00:09:33.486
If I'd really thought about this person,

00:09:33.528 --> 00:09:36.531
I would have spent far greater time
actually on their strengths,

00:09:36.531 --> 00:09:42.703
actually emphasizing why they were such a
valuable member of our organization,

00:09:42.703 --> 00:09:46.207
and then, creating a space
where they felt psychologically safe,

00:09:46.207 --> 00:09:49.335
to be able to go on and explore
these areas for development.

00:09:51.254 --> 00:09:54.215
Reflection has helped me to improve this

00:09:54.257 --> 00:09:56.884
and to build better relationships.

00:09:58.594 --> 00:10:01.180
So this might all sound
a little fine and dandy,

00:10:01.222 --> 00:10:04.600
and may be obvious or trivial,

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:09.689
but the truth is, so many of us don't take
time out for regular reflection.

00:10:12.108 --> 00:10:14.235
This practice has helped me

00:10:15.111 --> 00:10:16.779
and I am so grateful.

00:10:18.865 --> 00:10:21.284
After I left being a priest,

00:10:21.325 --> 00:10:25.830
I took some time out for reflection
to think about who I was

00:10:25.872 --> 00:10:28.040
and what I wanted my life to be about.

00:10:29.458 --> 00:10:31.752
Reflection helped me to grapple with this,

00:10:31.752 --> 00:10:33.462
and it continues to help me today

00:10:33.462 --> 00:10:36.215
as I grapple with this and other topics.

00:10:38.634 --> 00:10:44.682
So as we end this day, or tomorrow,
before you begin the next,

00:10:44.682 --> 00:10:46.392
sit down,

00:10:46.392 --> 00:10:47.685
take a breath,

00:10:47.685 --> 00:10:48.853
and reflect.

00:10:49.437 --> 00:10:52.440
And you'll see the power that this habit
brings to your life.

00:10:53.983 --> 00:10:55.151
Thank you.

00:10:55.193 --> 00:10:58.362
(Cheers and applause)

